\section{Conclusion}
Overlapping reserve categories create a genuine allocation problem because admitted students must be counted in exactly one category. Score constraints make feasibility depend on endogenous cutoffs, so non-wastefulness must be read relative to the score rule, and full capacity utilization can fail to exist. Within-category fairness, by contrast, survives unchanged, for the reason recorded in Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}.

We studied two mechanisms. SPSE combines common reserve-side score elevation with deferred-acceptance logic and keeps the initial reserve capacities fixed, and it inherits the full incentive and no-envy properties of deferred acceptance, but it need not satisfy score-feasibility. PA-ODR enforces the score constraint by filling the open category first and converting unfulfillable reserve capacity into open capacity when necessary; it retains within-category fairness in the unweakened sense, but it loses both strategy-proofness and freedom from justified envy. Table~\ref{tab:comparison} summarizes the comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Comparison of the two mechanisms. The two mechanisms agree on every row above the rule and differ on every row below it.}
\label{tab:comparison}
\centering
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{SPSE} & \textbf{PA-ODR} \\
\midrule
One-to-one counting & Yes & Yes \\
Feasibility & Yes & Yes, w.r.t.\ final $\hat q$ \\
Non-wastefulness & Yes & Yes, w.r.t.\ final $\hat q$ \\
Within-category fairness (Def.~\ref{def:fair}) & Yes & Yes \\
\midrule
Score-feasibility & Not guaranteed & Enforced by construction \\
No justified envy (Def.~\ref{def:nje}) & Yes & No (Ex.~\ref{ex:paodr-envy}) \\
Strategy-proofness & Yes & No (Prop.~\ref{prop:paodr-notsp}) \\
Capacity reallocation & Fixed at $q$ & Endogenous, not minimal \\
Uses category preferences & Through proposals & Through lexicographic tie-breaking \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The main design tension is therefore between fixed reserve capacities and score-feasibility. Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} shows that feasibility, non-wastefulness, and within-category fairness do not imply score-feasibility, while Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} shows that full open and reserve utilization can be impossible under the cutoff rule. PA-ODR restores score-feasibility by allowing capacity reallocation, but Example~\ref{ex:order} shows that this reallocation is order-dependent and need not minimize de-reservation. The table makes the shape of the trade-off explicit: score-feasibility is bought with the incentive and envy properties of deferred acceptance, and by Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} it cannot be bought merely by choosing a better mechanism with the capacities held fixed.

