Several questions remain. One is to characterize the instances on which SPSE is automatically score-feasible; Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} shows the class is proper, but not where its boundary lies. A second is whether the incentive loss of Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-notsp} is unavoidable: is there any score-feasible and strategy-proof mechanism in this environment, necessarily de-reserving, and if so at what cost in reserve capacity? A third is the complexity of \textsc{MinDeres} when $|T|$ is part of the input, for which Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes} gives only the fixed-parameter case. Finally, the model deliberately assumes a common bonus $k$; allowing category-specific bonuses would break the property that an overlapping student is admissible either for all of her categories or for none, which is what keeps the feasibility graph as simple as it is here.

